    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d351">

                <pb facs="0532" n="438" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg438"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">612.112/10: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Vera Cruz</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wood</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vera Cruz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-01-04T16:00:00-07:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 4,
                                1924—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 10:28
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>American interests desire Department’s position as to whether they can export and
                    import without being fined and treated as smugglers by the Obregon government in
                    the event of its return here. They refer to article number 6 of Mexican customs
                    regulations <hi rendition="#italic">Código de Procedimientos Aduaneros Ordenanza
                        General de Aduanas</hi>, pages 6 and 7, edition 1912. Respectfully request
                    instructions <pb facs="0533" n="439" xml:id="pg439"/> by telegraph as large sums
                    are involved covering coffee and sugar shipments awaiting reply.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wood</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
